From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 00:13:33 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: configs: asahi: make transparent huge pages
 the default

With 16K pages a transparent huge page maps 32 MiB through one TLB
entry. Measured on the J604 (M4, 1.880): a random pointer chase over
512 MiB takes 106.8 ns per load with THP on madvise, as built, and
97.3 ns with it set to always, the same as on 32 MiB hugetlb pages. On
madvise almost nothing gets one, as few programs ask: the gap to macOS
(91.3 ns) stays at 15.5 ns instead of 6 ns. Bandwidth is unchanged.

Default to always. Defrag stays at madvise, so a fault that finds no
free 32 MiB block takes base pages and never stalls on compaction, and
the page block cap above is unaffected. Both stay runtime switches in
/sys/kernel/mm/transparent_hugepage.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
---
 arch/arm64/configs/asahi.config | 5 +++--
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/arch/arm64/configs/asahi.config b/arch/arm64/configs/asahi.config
index 60c4e09064..e25019d9be 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/configs/asahi.config
+++ b/arch/arm64/configs/asahi.config
@@ -14,9 +14,10 @@ CONFIG_ARM64_MEMORY_MODEL_CONTROL=y
 # 16K pages make a page block 32 MiB, and khugepaged then sets min_free_kbytes
 # to 352 MiB on an 8 GB Mac. Keep page blocks at 2 MiB as on 4K kernels.
 CONFIG_PAGE_BLOCK_MAX_ORDER=7
-# THP for memory that asks for it (madvise), which the cap above assumes.
+# THP by default: one 32 MiB TLB entry per huge page. Defrag stays madvise, so
+# faults never stall on compaction and the page block cap above still holds.
 CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE=y
-CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE_MADVISE=y
+CONFIG_TRANSPARENT_HUGEPAGE_ALWAYS=y
 CONFIG_ARM_APPLE_CPUIDLE=y
 CONFIG_ARM_APPLE_SOC_CPUFREQ=m
 # The CLPC's power modes are platform profiles, and the platform_profile
